# libpwquality -- the password quality check behind gnome-control-center's
# Users panel, and the pam_pwquality module that enforces it at password
# change.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/libpwquality/libpwquality/releases/download/libpwquality-1.4.5/libpwquality-1.4.5.tar.bz2
SRC_SHA256=6fcf18b75d305d99d04d2e42982ed5b787a081af2842220ed63287a2d6a10988
SRC_DIR=libpwquality-1.4.5

# THE CRACKLIB CHECK IS ON, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT AND ALSO A CHOICE.
# configure.ac:161 offers --disable-cracklib-check, and without it line 175 is a
# hard AC_MSG_ERROR([No or unusable cracklib library]) -- so this package is
# buildable either way and the dictionary is not forced on anyone.
#
# It is on because the alternative is ACTIVE FALSE ASSURANCE. With the check
# disabled libpwquality still applies its own rules -- length, character
# classes, palindromes, sequences, similarity to the username -- so it is not
# useless. But gnome-control-center's Users panel draws the SAME strength bar
# either way and cannot tell the user which check ran, so a build without the
# dictionary reports a password as vetted when the dictionary lookup never
# happened. That is the one shape this tree does not ship.
#
# See pkgs/cracklib, where the dictionary is generated at build time and
# asserted three ways, because a cracklib whose dictionary is missing does not
# error -- FascistCheck simply returns "no problem".
#
# --disable-python-bindings. The option DEFAULTS YES (configure.ac:124-129), so
# doing nothing builds a Python binding nothing in this tree consumes -- the
# same auto-on-by-default shape as cracklib's own --with-python.
#
# --enable-pam is stated rather than left at its default of `check`, which
# autodetects and silently produces no PAM module if libpam is absent. The
# module is the half that enforces the policy at password change; the library
# alone only lets an application ask. With the flag explicit, a builder image
# without PAM fails here instead of shipping a quieter package.
#
# --sysconfdir=/etc IS LOAD-BEARING, and for two reasons rather than one.
# configure.ac:118 sets SCONFIGDIR to $sysconfdir/security, so the generic
# build.sh's --prefix=/usr would put pwquality.conf under /usr/etc/security --
# installed, correct-looking, and read by nothing. That is the gnome-menus trap
# again, three recipes later.
#
# AND THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE DOES NOT WORK: --with-sconfigdir looks like the
# targeted fix and configure.ac:118 reads `SCONFIGDIR=$enableval` -- $enableval,
# in an AC_ARG_WITH block, where the value arrives as $withval. So the flag
# silently sets the directory to an empty string instead of the path given. An
# upstream bug, and the reason the fix here is the general option rather than
# the specific one.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static \
--sysconfdir=/etc \
--enable-pam \
--disable-python-bindings \
--with-securedir=/usr/lib/security"
